  "post": {
    "caption": "Pearl necklaces are full of tiny knots. Most pearl necklaces have a knot between each pearl, so if the strand breaks, usually only one pearl comes loose, and the knots also keep pearls from rubbing against each other. Pearls are often strung on silk, but silk stretches and weakens, so a strand worn often may need restringing about once a year. Stringers tie each knot by hand with fine tweezers or an awl, and French wire, also called bullion, often protects the thread at the clasp. Pearls are drilled, and the cord is sized to the hole. A princess length strand is about 17 to 19 inches.",
